   "source": [
    "### Q13: Suppose you tried to build a $c$ chart on the Back-Issue Defect Counts data instead of a $u$ chart. Why would this not be an appropriate choice, given how the data were collected?"
   ]
  },A c chart assumes every sample covers the same area of opportunity, so the raw defect count is comparable from week to week. The back-issue data violate that assumption because staff inspected a different number of boxes each week (anywhere from 2 to 10), whenever they had time. A week with 10 boxes inspected will naturally produce more defects than a week with 2 boxes, even if the quality of the boxes is identical.
